this example says find the coefficient  of the X cubed Y to the fifth term in  the expansion of 2x minus 3y to the  eighth power now you can imagine if we  were to write this 8 times and you have  to multiply it all out and combine like  terms that would take quite a bit of  time right so that's where the binomial  expansion theorem comes in and  specifically we're going to use that to  find out just that one specific term and  go ahead and find the number okay in  front of that X cubed Y to the fifth  term so first things first let's review  a couple things first thing is Pascal's  triangle and you can see with Pascal's  triangle that the rows begin with 1 and  they end with 1 ok so the rows go like  this right and then to get the term  below what you do is you add the two  terms above us we'll see how 1 plus 2  adds up to 3 two plus one adds up to 3  or 1 + 3 adds up to 4 now another way to  get these terms okay is to use this  combination formula so out of n items  how many ways other to choose R items  and the formula is n factorial over n  minus R factorial R factorial and so for  example if I'm down here on the fourth  row notice it starts at row 0 1 2 3 4  this number right here is actually 4  choose 0 so how many ways are to choose  0 out of 4 there's only one way to do  that and the same thing here this is 4  choose 1 4 choose 2 4 choose 3 all the  way over here 2 4 choose 4 so it's  always the row number choose 0 all the  way up through the row number choose  whatever row number you're on so for  example say we wanted to expand a plus B  to the third power what we can do is we  could say well let's go down here to the  third row and these are our coefficients  1 3 3 1 the other way to do it is to go  like this we could say 3 2 0 3 choose 1  3 choose 2 3 choose 3 then what you do  is you take the first term okay here a  and you go to the third power and you go  in descending order a squared a to the  first a to the 0 then you take the  second term B and you go in ascending  order so B to the 0 B to the first and B  to the second and B to the third and  then these terms are all added together  and you can go ahead and simplify and  those are going to be the terms in the  expansion  a plus B to the third power now I want  you to notice a pattern here that will  be very helpful see how we're on the 3rd  power we're also on the third row here  see this 3 2 0 notice how the second  term see the B B to the 0 power and see  how this is 3 choose 0 see how these  guys match same thing over here C 3  choose 1 B to the first power C 1 in 1  the other thing I want you to notice too  is that see how 3 plus 0 adds up to 3  that's the power here that's also the  row ok 2 plus 1 is 3 1 plus 2 is 3 so if  you recognize that pattern you can use  that to find a specific term in this  expansion so I'll show you over here so  for example 2x minus 3y to the 8th we  can see that our two terms are 2x and  negative 3y now I'm capturing this minus  as a negative 3y and then we want this  to be X cubes I'm going to make this to  the third power I'm going to make this  to the fifth power to give us Y to the  fifth  now notice we're on the eighth power so  that's the 8th roll through were to  expand this down Pascal's triangle this  is going to be 8 choose what do you  think goes here well remember the  pattern c 0 0 1 1 2 2 and so on so if  this is 5 this is going to be 5 as well  so now all we have to do is we're going  to use the formula over here for  combinations ok so 8 choose 5 that's 8  factorial over 8 minus 5 3 factorial  times 5 factorial and then we've got 2 X  cubed which is 2 cubed is 8 okay  negative 3 to the 5th is negative 243 Y  to the fifth now all we have to do now  is simplify all the numbers and that's  going to be our coefficient in front of  the X cubed Y to the fifth term so 8  factorial is really like 8 times 7 times  6 times 5 all the way down to 1 5  factorial is 5 4 3 2 multiplying all the  way down to one so you can see those are  cancelling 3 factorial is 3 times 2  times 1 which is 6 so you can see those  are cancelling so now we can see we have  8 times 7 which is 56 times 8 times  negative 243 so let's go to the  calculator here we've got 56 times 8  times negative 243 so that comes out to  neg  108,000 negative one hundred eight  thousand eight hundred and sixty-four X  cubed Y to the fifth now if they just  want that coefficient you would just  write down the negative one hundred  eight thousand eight hundred and  sixty-four so we're gonna do another  example but I know a lot of students  right now are preparing for the AC T and  the SAT especially in a math section so  I just want to let you know about my two  math review courses I got a huge AC t--  math review video course and a huge SAT  math review video course so check out  those video courses there's three free  lessons in each of those courses so if  you take those you'll get a lot of  benefit just from going through those  lessons and you'll get an idea about  what the other lessons are going to be  like as well and if you're interested  you can go on and purchase that and go  through the rest of those a lot of  students have reported back that they've  been able to boost their scores  utilizing those courses that I put  together so check them out otherwise  let's go to example number two and says  find the coefficient of the X to the  fourth Y to the twelfth term in this  expansion  okay so we're already little bit more  experienced now so we say hmm X to the  fourth Y to the twelfth well you can see  these are our two terms right here right  in our binomial by means two right so  five x squared and negative two Y to the  third I want this to be X to the fourth  that means this has to be two because a  power to power you multiply that's going  to give us the X to the fourth this I  want to be four because Y cubed to the  fourth power three times four gives us  twelve we're on the sixth row sixth  power so this is going to be six choose  and remember the pattern see how this is  three two zero B to the zero 3 choose  one B to the one this is going to be 6  choose 4 see how these match now all we  have to do is simplify this out and  that's going to be the particular term  that we're looking for so let's do that  so 6 choose 4 we use our binomial I'm  sorry our combination form the right up  there we've got 6 factorial over 6 minus  4 2 factorial times 4 factorial 5  squared is 25 X to the fourth negative 2  to the fourth is 16 y to the 12th and  let's simplify this so 6 factorial is 6  times 5 times 4 all the way down to 1 4  factorial is 4 all the way down to one  so those are cancelling and then 2  factorial  just two times one so this is 30 over 2  which is 15 times 25 times 16 X to the  fourth Y to the 12th now we're only  interested in the coefficient so we just  have to multiply those values out right  there so that's going to be 15 times 25  times 16 let's see that's 6,000 so our  coefficient in this particular example  is going to be 6,000 so I hope that  helped you how to understand working  with binomial expansion theorem a little  bit better I have another video that I  can link to too if you want to do more  problems with binomial expansion but  this is specifically focused on finding  the coefficient so I hope that helps you  understand a lot better  subscribe to the channel check out more  math videos on my youtube channel  Mario's math tutoring and I look forward  to helping you in the future math videos  I'll talk to you soon

